<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="pyridoxal 5&apos;-phosphate salvage II (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7204" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Vitamin B6 is a group term for |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL| (PL), |FRAME:PYRIDOXINE| (PN), |FRAME:PYRIDOXAMINE| (PM) and their 5&apos;-phosphorylated derivatives |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL_PHOSPHATE| (PLP), |FRAME:PYRIDOXINE-5P| (PNP) and |FRAME:PYRIDOXAMINE-5P| (PMP) which are also referred to as vitamers. Plants, fungi and bacteria possess the biosynthetic capability to produce vitamin B6 via a &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; PLP biosynthesis |CITS:[17822383]| however auxotrophic organisms such as mammalians require the external uptake of the non-phosphorylated vitamers which are then being converted to PLP in the intracellular vitamin B6 salvage pathway |CITS:[15483325]|. |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL_PHOSPHATE Pyridoxal 5&apos;-phosphate| is considered the active form of vitamin B6 and is involved as essential cofactor in amino acid biosynthetic pathways |CITS:[7840669]| |CITS:[11402193]| |CITS:[11344332]| and a diversity of other enzymatic reactions |CITS:[17512961]| |CITS:[19145213]|. In plants, PLP is known as cofactor of various key enzymes which play a role in ethylene biosynthesis |CITS:[10557240]|, chlorophyll and tetrapyrrole synthesis |CITS:[12767809]|, &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; sphingolipid biosynthesis |CITS:[11171191]| and carbohydrate metabolism |CITS:[6778873]|. Moreover, vitamers have been shown to have antioxidant properties impeding the formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and are considered to be involved in stress response pathways |CITS:[10687384]| |CITS:[17227548]| |CITS:[19903353]| |CITS:[19145213]|. The vitamin B6 salvage pathway for plants is very similar to the bacterial variant (see |FRAME:PLPSAL-PWY|). Three enzymes from plants catalyzing main reactions in the salvage pathway have been characterized so far. The non-phophorylated vitamers PL, PN, and PM are phosphorylated to PLP, PNP, and PMP through pyridoxal kinase(s). So far the phosphorylation of |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL| to |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL_PHOSPHATE| has been demonstrated in wheat and |FRAME:TAX-3701|. Although not tested as substrates in these experiments it is expected that the pyridoxal kinase works likewise with |FRAME:PYRIDOXINE| and |FRAME:PYRIDOXAMINE| |CITS:[12244454]| |CITS:[15499907]|. The reverse reactions catalyzed by phosphatases have been suggested based on homologues of microbial and animal genes that exist in plant genomes but no enzymes have been reported yet. The formed phosphorylated vitamers, i.e. |FRAME:PYRIDOXINE-5P| and |FRAME:PYRIDOXAMINE-5P| are converted to |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL_PHOSPHATE| by the catalytic action of pyridoxine/pyrimidine 5&apos;-phosphate oxidases |CITS:[17224143]| |CITS:[7138019]|. In addition, a pyridoxal reductase from |FRAME:TAX-3701| has been purified that catalyzes the NADPH-dependent reduction of |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL| to |FRAME:PYRIDOXINE| |CITS:[21533842]|. Hence, it appears that plants, with the vitamin B6 salvage pathway, maintain pools of the various vitamers and possess the ability to generate and recycle non-phosphorylated and phosphorylated forms which upon demand generate the active vitamin B6 |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL_PHOSPHATE|.</Description>
