      <Xref identifier="Q56Y42" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="495" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="O-28, hartmut">The enzymatic activity of a pyridoxal reductase was identified in Arabidopsis using the homolog of the known enzyme from yeast. The cDNA was cloned and expressed in yeast (&lt;I&gt;|FRAME:TAX-4932 &lt;i&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae&lt;/i&gt;|&lt;/i&gt;). The protein could not be purified via affinity column chromatography as it partitioned mostly with the insoluble fraction. However, the crude extract of the yeast showed the capability to catalyze the reduction of |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL pyridoxal| to generate |FRAME:PYRIDOXINE pyridoxine|. The reverse reaction (|FRAME:PYRIDOXINE pyridoxine| -&gt; |FRAME:PYRIDOXAL pyridoxal|) as known from yeast could not be observed. In addition, the identified gene (At5g53580) when expressed in the background of a PLR1 knock-out mutant of yeast (Sc-KO) was shown to partially rescue the SC-KO phenotype by restoring pyridoxal reductase activity. Furthermore, the T-DNA PLR1 mutant of |FRAME:TAX-3701 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;| plr1-1 had increased activities of two other vitamin B salvage plant enzymes (PDX3, SOS4). Vice versa PDX3 and SOS4 deficient mutants (sos4, pdx3-1, pdx3-2) showed an increased expression of PLR1 whereas genes of the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis of vitamin B remained unaffected. That indicates a cooperative behavior of PLR1 with the other known vitamin B6 salvage genes emphasizing its association with this pathway |CITS:[21533842]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="MONOMER-17899" />
